  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures are expected\nthrough early next week.\n\nThe pattern will remain relatively stagnant through tomorrow, \nwith a Nor'easter continuing to circulate off the Mid- Atlantic \ncoast and high pressure gradually building to our north. \nNortherly winds will continue to advect dry air into the region,\nleading to clear skies. Temperatures will be near seasonal \nnormals, with highs in the 60s to near 70 and lows in the mid to\nupper 30s (west of the Blue Ridge) to lower to middle 40s (east\nof the Blue Ridge).\n\nBy Thursday, what remains of the upper reflection of the offshore \nclosed low gradually becomes absorbed into a deep trough over \neastern Canada. These adjoining features are slated to pull eastward \ninto the Canadian Maritimes while upstream heights build across the \nMississippi Valley. From Friday and into this weekend, this broad \nridge axis advances eastward ahead of a trough inhabiting the \nwestern/central U.S. As this occurs, there are some hints of another \nupper low center getting pinched off near the Carolina coast. \nHowever, its far southward displacement should minimize any direct \nimpacts from this system. Overall synoptic uncertainty does increase \nmarkedly toward the end of the weekend and into early next week. \nMost notably, it revolves around a split in the jet streams over the \nmiddle of the continent. \n\nLooking down at the surface, high pressure dominates through late \nweek while becoming more centered over the northeastern U.S. by \nSaturday. This ensures continued tranquil weather heading toward the \nlast week of October. Expect dry conditions along with abundant \nsunshine each day. The air mass will remain quite dry owing to the \nprevailing northerly component to the local wind fields. Most days \nshould yield highs in the 60s (50s for the mountains). The coldest \novernight periods will likely be Friday and Saturday nights as lows \nfall into the 30s to low 40s. With the growing season having ended \nfrom the Blue Ridge westward, it remains to be seen if any \nfrost/freeze threats emerge off to the east.",
